      "side": 1,
      "message": "Sorry about the poor example earlier – I’ve fixed it now. The new case involves storing vector elements in a matrix within a struct:\n\nThe struct is defined as:\nS_mat3x4h_align \u003d struct @align(8) { x:u32 @offset(0); y:mat3x4\u003cf16\u003e @offset(8) }\n\nAccording to the original logic, SetBaseEleType(var) picks U32 as the base element type for this struct. In MakeVectorStore, this leads to ratio \u003d st_ele_ty-\u003eSize() / BaseEleType()-\u003eSize() \u003d 2/4 \u003d 0, which fails the TINT_IR_ASSERT(ir, ratio \u003d\u003d 1 || ratio \u003d\u003d 2) check.\n\nAdditionally, using u32 to store f16 may introduce data races for scalar f16 writes – two adjacent f16 values written by different invocations would hit the same u32 value.\n\nI plan to adjust the code logic for selecting the base element type. My current thought is: if the vector contains f16, have SmallestElementSize return 2, so SetBaseEleType can choose u16 as the base element type instead.\n\nDo you have any better suggestions for this fix? Thanks!",
